WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.463
Why does this chapter have
an end date built in?

2
00:00:02.463 --> 00:00:06.604
Because a group managed service account
only works while A D is in charge,

3
00:00:06.604 --> 00:00:09.779
and anything still running
under one of these accounts

4
00:00:09.779 --> 00:00:13.162
loses its credential when the
last domain controller goes.

5
00:00:13.162 --> 00:00:17.244
So every account must end up
moving to a workload identity,

6
00:00:17.244 --> 00:00:19.625
retiring, or on a dated island list.

7
00:00:19.625 --> 00:00:22.160
The program's rules, as design guidance:

8
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.437
each account's disposition is the
owner's decision from Chapter 3.

9
00:00:26.437 --> 00:00:29.810
New accounts follow the managed
service account standard,

10
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:33.113
and a group account's retrievers
are one group of its hosts.

11
00:00:33.113 --> 00:00:37.300
Every write carries its SailPoint
request and ServiceNow change.

12
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.938
The pipeline's own
account sends what it can;

13
00:00:39.938 --> 00:00:42.575
a person sends the
delegated account steps.

14
00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:45.878
And never delete: the old
account is disabled and

15
00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:49.320
moved to a holding O U, and
a person deletes it later.
